== In pop culture ==
The University of Chicago is the [[alma mater]] of fictional characters Harry Burns and Sally Albright (from ''[[When Harry Met Sally...|When Harry Met Sally]]''),<ref>{{Cite news |last=Weber |first=Bruce |date=1989-07-09 |title=FILM; Can Men and Women Be Friends? |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1989/07/09/movies/film-can-men-and-women-be-friends.html |access-date=2026-02-24 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> [[Indiana Jones]], and Mark Watney (from ''[[The Martian (Weir novel)|The Martian]]''). It has served as a filming location for scenes in ''[[Divergent (film)|Divergent]],'' ''[[The Fugitive (1993 film)|The Fugitive]]'', and ''[[Sense8]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Chicago in TV and Movies |url=https://collegeadmissions.uchicago.edu/uncommon-blog/chicago-tv-and-movies |url-status=dead |access-date=May 9, 2025 |website=[[University of Chicago]] |language=en |archive-date=September 7, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240907144435/https://collegeadmissions.uchicago.edu/uncommon-blog/chicago-tv-and-movies }}</ref>

Abe Ravelstein, the titular character of the novel ''[[Ravelstein]],'' was based on UChicago faculty member [[Allan Bloom]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=With Friends Like Saul Bellow |url=https://archive.nytimes.com/www.nytimes.com/library/magazine/home/20000416mag-ravelstein.html |access-date=May 26, 2025 |website=archive.nytimes.com}}</ref>

